WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.325
How to build an Agentforce agent in Zana.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:10.038
Use Pi with the SF-Pi plugin. It builds,
previews, and tests the agent for you.

00:00:11.301 --> 00:00:17.333
Start a thread on the Pi harness, in your Salesforce
project, and describe the agent you want.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:24.140
Pi plans it, writes the Agent Script,
and compiles it, right in the thread.

00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:26.585
It hands back a card for the new agent.

00:00:26.765 --> 00:00:32.860
Click it, and the agent opens beside the
thread: the script, and its conversation graph.

00:00:33.041 --> 00:00:38.346
Ask what the return window is. Pi
previews the agent and links the run.

00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:44.800
The run opens in the same panel.
Fourteen days. Our policy is thirty.

00:00:44.981 --> 00:00:52.111
Ask for thirty days, and five test scenarios. Pi
edits the file, then writes the scenarios itself.

00:00:52.292 --> 00:00:54.829
Open it again, and the script reads thirty.

00:00:55.009 --> 00:01:01.612
Pi runs the five scenarios as simulated
customers, four at a time. Five of five pass.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:04.754
Check it yourself, right in the panel: thirty days.

00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:15.148
When you want the full workbench, open it in Agentforce
Studio: the same script, thirty days and all.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:21.278
Every action is one click away, like the
return Flow, drawn as its real canvas.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:26.168
And the Lightning Type the order lookup
returns, with the card that renders it.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.186
Built, previewed, and tested, from one conversation.
